To perform specific numerical computations, we need to assume probability distributions for each random variable. In this study, we select probability distributions that are natural, simple, and easy to handle as suitable choices for the physical model. The probability distributions that random variables follow have degrees of freedom of choice, which will be discussed in Sect. 5. Considering the magnitude of external shocks, we assume that larger shocks are less likely to occur, following an exponential distribution. Furthermore, since the occurrence of external shocks is considered random, the number of occurrences follows a Poisson distribution. Thus, we have the following as probability density functions, respectively,8\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
