Brillinger (1961) was the first to point out that, when the human body constitutes a serial system comprising numerous independent and identically distributed components, increasing the number of these components sufficiently leads to the result of Fisher-Tippett’s theorem2 in Extreme Value Theory (EVT), resulting in the Gompertz law, \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
