				\begin{document}$$\alpha u$$\end{document}αu and n. The mortality model discussed in this study address the decline in physiological function of individual organisms due to the accumulation of external shocks. Therefore, it may be desirable to compare the model with longitudinal life tables, namely cohort life tables. However, cohort life tables, which track the same cohort of individuals until death for nearly 100 years, have limitations in terms of data availability. Furthermore, as explained later, the prolongation of lifespan in advanced countries is the result of two different mechanisms of lifespan transformation in the first and second halves of the 20th century, and the mortarity model is more suitable for the latter. Hence, considering the practical limitations of cohort life tables, we chose to focus on relatively recent data by using census life tables. Regarding the use of the Human Mortality Database, we utilised life table data from 2019 (for males in Japan and the UK), unaffected by the mortality impact of the Covid-19 infection, which became widespread globally after 2020. We would like to elaborate on the range of estimated parameter values and their corresponding physical meanings below.

Figure 3 depicts the probability density functions of mortality and the survival curves from 1950 to 2010 in Japan and the UK, respectively. It has been pointed out in several papers (Horiuchi 2001; Olshansky and Ault 1986; McDonald 2019) that the extension of lifespan in developed countries in the 20th century is the result of two different types of lifespan transitions. The first type is the decline in mortality rates, mainly in infants and young adults, due to factors such as infectious diseases and malnutrition, and the second type is the decline in mortality rates in middle-aged and elderly individuals, primarily due to circulatory system diseases such as heart disease and cerebrovascular diseases. The second type is still ongoing. If we observe these changes in survival curves, the first type contributes to a rectangularisation, while the second type contributes to a shift in the survival curve towards older ages. In other words, the two types are based on different mechanisms, and the physical model of ageing in this study, which focuses on the deterioration of physiological functions, is considered to be more suitable for the second one.Fig. 3
